[{:seon.task/name "one-derivation"
  :seon.task/plan "Slice 1 of the one-derivation design (docs/render.md, revision 3, root's approval of 2026-10-26): @ctx is the one derivation, and the page, the request and the agent's own view are its renders. This slice is ctx's side (docs/ctx.md#kept-by-the-change-never-rebuilt). seon.ctx/build takes any entity (an agent's, root's, the user's, a task's), and seon.ctx/follow patches it from the writer's commits of main. seon.ctx/patch answers :changed, the paths a transaction changed, keeping every other path identical? for a part's memo. ctx holds the db's data only (owner, 2026-10-26: no derived data in the db or the ctx atom): the task's data (seon.context/task-data, its memo key task-key), related (seon.context/related) and an entry's first transaction (seon.context/first-t) are renders a view reads the db through (seon.context/renders); a catch-up is a fact of the agent's record. It replaces nothing yet: the walk and the request's own reads are deleted by later slices, once views read ctx."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.ctx-test/ctx-is-the-record-after-every-transaction seon.ctx-test/any-entitys-record-follows-mains-commits seon.ctx-test/the-patch-costs-the-change-not-the-session seon.context-test/the-task-key-moves-with-what-it-reads seon.context-cost-test/a-task-key-costs-what-the-plan-names seon.agent-test/after-merge-main-the-agent-judges-against-the-new-base}}]
